['Yes, I can help you implement TCP in TypeScript.

Here's an example implementation of TCP in TypeScript:
```typescript
class Tcp {
  constructor(private readonly socket: Socket) {}

  send(data: Uint8Array): Promise<void> {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      this.socket.send(data, 0, data.length, (err) => {
        if (err) {
          reject(err);
        } else {
          resolve();
        }
      });
    });
  }

  recv(length: number): Promise<Uint8Array> {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      this.socket.on("data", (data: Uint8Array) => {
        if (data.length === 0) {
          reject(new Error("socket closed"));
        } else {
          resolve(data);
        }
      });

      this.socket.on("end", () => {
        resolve(new Uint8Array());
      });

      let received = 0;
      this.socket.on("data", (data: Uint8Array) => {
        received += data.length;
        if (received === length) {
          resolve(data);
        }
      });
    });
  }
}
```
In this example, the `Tcp` class has two methods: `send` and `recv`. The `send` method takes a `Uint8Array` of data to send over the TCP connection, and returns a Promise that resolves when the send operation is complete. The `recv` method takes a `length` parameter that indicates the maximum number of bytes to expect to receive in each call to `recv`, and returns a Promise that resolves with the received data as a `Uint8Array`.

Note that this is a simple example and does not handle all possible error cases or edge cases. You may want to modify it to fit your specific needs.']

